artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $(M,g)$ be a four-dimensional smooth Lorentzian manifold serving as a spacetime in general relativity, with metric $g_{\mu\nu}$ satisfying the Einstein field equations $G_{\mu\nu}[g] = 8\pi G\, T_{\mu\nu}$, where $G_{\mu\nu}[g]$ is the Einstein tensor and $T_{\mu\nu}$ is the energy–momentum tensor of matter fields defined on $M$.

Provide a mathematically rigorous, generally covariant definition of an averaging (coarse-graining) operation that takes as input the geometric data $g_{\mu\nu}$ and the matter data $T_{\mu\nu}$ on $M$ at a "fine" scale and produces, on the same differentiable manifold (or a quotient/refinement thereof), coarse-grained tensor fields $\langle g_{\mu\nu}\rangle$ and $\langle T_{\mu\nu}\rangle$ such that:

(i) the averaging procedure is independent of the choice of coordinates and respects the tensorial/covariant character of the fields being averaged (in particular, the average of a tensor field is a well-defined tensor field);
(ii) the procedure is defined on a generic differentiable manifold without requiring auxiliary background structure (such as a preferred foliation, Killing symmetries, or a fixed background metric) beyond what can be canonically constructed;
(iii) it specifies how the nonlinear Einstein equations transform under the averaging, yielding a well-posed system of equations governing the coarse-grained fields, with explicit "backreaction" or correlation terms accounting for the failure of averaging to commute with the Einstein operator;
(iv) in appropriate limits (e.g. high-symmetry or near-homogeneous regimes) it reduces to the expected naive averages.

Construct such a framework, or prove that no construction satisfying (i)–(iv) can exist.

